WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:08.059
Have you ever seen a photo of a planet made
entirely of living flesh?

00:00:08.059 --> 00:00:11.190
Or an insect-human hybrid that can control
minds?

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:17.180
Or a thousand-foot-long sea serpent that emerges
in Iceland every year?

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:22.650
These images might look like they come from
some long-lost paranormal case file.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:28.449
But this is actually the work of the brilliant
artist and worldbuilder Eduardo Valdés-Hevia,

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:34.050
who has spent the last several years bringing
to life a menagerie of nightmares so detailed

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:35.870
that I had to know more…

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:41.270
The Archivist: “So, what would you say inspires
your art?”

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.410
Valdés-Hevia: “I’m a really big fan of
the found-footage genre.

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:49.461
The type of horror that is not presented directly
as if you’re the subject, or you’re being

00:00:49.461 --> 00:00:50.850
chased by this monster.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.270
You’ve just found the diary of a person
that was chased by that monster.

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:59.829
And you are living that experience through
those documents…”

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:05.570
Brought to life through a combination of old
photographs and new elements, Valdés-Hevia’s

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:11.570
art merges history and the surreal, with images
that feel like they come directly from classified

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:13.530
historical documents.

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:20.020
The artist creates stand-alone images as well
as richly detailed series with dense, interconnected

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:21.020
lore.

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:26.530
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
investigate the monsters and madness of these

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.450
dark case files.

00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:33.049
And you can support the artist on Patreon
and follow the projects using the links in

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:34.220
the description.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:40.130
Now, prepare yourself for the terrors of Valdés-Hevia…

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:49.840
Valdés-Hevia’s longest project is the Megalamorpha
series, a fragmented anthology exploring humanity’s

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:55.380
many unfortunate encounters with mysterious,
insect-like lifeforms.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.330
The Archivist: “What inspired the Megalamorpha
project?”

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:02.390
Valdés-Hevia: “I kind of modeled this story
after internet rabbit holes.

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:04.200
So, it’s a bunch of small stories.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.119
In each of these stories there’s like one
piece of the puzzle — of that larger lore

00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:09.539
— but only one.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:14.950
You need to read a bunch of them to start
putting them together.”

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:21.110
One of the earliest fragments of the Megalamorpha
story comes from 1910 on the coast of Maine.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:27.860
An unidentified carcass washes up on shore
— a strange shell that resembles a giant

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:28.860
insect.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.510
Shortly after, the town’s fishermen who
discover it become paranoid that the abnormal

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:40.959
lifeform is cursed — and burn the remains
before scientists can examine them.

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:48.409
Another sighting comes in 1913, when the Australasian
Antarctic Expedition finds something strange

00:02:48.409 --> 00:02:50.159
in the ice.

00:02:50.159 --> 00:02:54.760
Recovered diary entries indicate that the
crew extracted the frozen lifeform from the

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:57.650
glacier and brought it back to camp.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:03.409
Unfortunately, the specimen would be lost
later that winter, when crewman Sidney Jeffryes

00:03:03.409 --> 00:03:08.310
came to believe the creature was trapped,
and wanted him to break it free.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:14.209
In the end, he shattered the frozen block
with an axe, destroying the specimen within…

00:03:14.209 --> 00:03:19.780
Valdés-Hevia: “The Megalamorpha, they can
kind of make themselves appear to the people

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:22.480
around them as whatever they interpret.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.779
And it’s based on their previous beliefs.

00:03:24.779 --> 00:03:25.779
What their background is.

00:03:25.779 --> 00:03:28.489
They might think it’s some sort of deity…”

00:03:28.489 --> 00:03:34.591
A recovered poster shows that a few years
later in the 1920s, the Turner Bros. Circus

00:03:34.591 --> 00:03:38.189
began touring the USA with an unusual exhibit.

00:03:38.189 --> 00:03:44.900
They claimed it was a species of ‘Amazonian
Mammoth Ant,’ although no such genus exists.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:50.569
One of the two founders of the circus allegedly
became obsessed with the mysterious insect,

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.269
and began sleeping in the cage with it.

00:03:53.269 --> 00:03:59.629
Eventually, he escaped with the ant, never
to be seen again...

00:03:59.629 --> 00:04:06.379
A leaked report tells us that nearly a century
later in June of 1961, a group of Russian

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:11.819
navy personnel discovered something unsettling
in the Kara Sea.

00:04:11.819 --> 00:04:17.489
Upon investigating a sunken merchant ship,
a diver came upon the nest of some sort of

00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:18.489
creature.

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:24.500
Analysis of the eggs determined they belonged
to a species previously unknown to science.

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:28.930
But while the diver’s camera was recovered,
the diver was not.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:34.139
The rest of the crew deemed the waters unsafe
to investigate further, and what eventually

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:39.349
became of the site remains a mystery.

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:44.259
Researcher Nigel Buckley tracked down the
details for most of the Megalamorpha sightings

00:04:44.259 --> 00:04:49.000
, making it his life’s work to collect the
scattered reports on the creatures before

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:52.610
passing away in 1986.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:57.409
Fragments of notes and illustrations indicate
Buckley believed the lifeforms were related

00:04:57.409 --> 00:04:58.760
to insects.

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:04.210
Despite the Megalamorpha’s extreme variability
in appearance, Buckley theorized they were

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:09.440
a single species that went through radical
metamorphosis over their life cycle.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.400
While Buckley could not prove the existence
of these nightmares, the sightings continued

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:15.720
after his death.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.960
But we’ll come back to the Megalamorpha…

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:26.550
Another brilliant horror series is The Façade
— a paranoia-inducing work that comes from

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:31.009
a collaboration between Valdés-Hevia and
fellow artist Luke Baker.

00:05:31.009 --> 00:05:34.611
Valdés-Hevia: “I love Luke’s art, so
we were chatting one day and I was like: we

00:05:34.611 --> 00:05:36.270
should collab on something.

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:40.639
We converged on the topic of doppelgangers.”

00:05:40.639 --> 00:05:44.009
The story begins in 1865.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:49.660
A photographer reveals his latest invention
to the French Emperor’s court: the Turning

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:50.660
portrait.

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:55.090
Yet the portrait of one Baron Renard comes
out… wrong.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.490
The camera is deemed defective.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:02.880
Unbeknownst to the court, the real problem
is that the individual in the photograph isn’t

00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.510
actually Renard.

00:06:05.510 --> 00:06:11.919
The next sighting of this lifeform — known
as the Façade — comes in 1896, when during

00:06:11.919 --> 00:06:16.500
the X-ray craze, people buy machines to show
to party guests.

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:22.180
In one such event in Hamburg, financier Franz
Mansfeld has this plate taken, revealing him

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:23.730
to be the Façade.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:28.300
He keeps the print a secret until vanishing
years later.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:34.740
Decades pass without sightings until 1960,
when a 6-year-old from Chicago is admitted

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.180
to the Lurie Children’s Hospital.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:42.270
In a crayon drawing, she claims there is a
monster pretending to be her mother.

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:47.700
Her records show another breakdown at age
23, thought to be at the time when the Façade

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:49.249
chose a new victim.

00:06:49.249 --> 00:06:51.520
Valdés-Hevia: “It changes identities.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:57.580
Every time it’s found out, it’ll go out,
kill someone else, and become them.”

00:06:57.580 --> 00:07:04.802
The last sighting comes in 1977, when a bystander
reports to the Chicago PD that he saw a man

00:07:04.802 --> 00:07:08.030
with a shifting face swallow someone whole.

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:13.270
His story wasn’t believed, although a single
police sketch was made of the suspect.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:22.020
The report was not investigated, and the Façade’s
current location remains unknown…

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:27.240
Many of Valdés-Hevia’s most striking pieces
don’t belong to a series, but are stand

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.360
alone works.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.219
Like this photograph of a group of people
watching the emergence of the ‘Niddhogg.’

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:40.810
“The whole idea is this huge creature, the
Niddhogg, every year explodes from the ground.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:45.259
But I want to set it into a world where it’s
a completely normal thing and everybody is

00:07:45.259 --> 00:07:46.259
used to it.”

00:07:46.259 --> 00:07:52.029
The name Niddhogg comes from Norse mythology,
and belongs to a dragon that gnaws at a root

00:07:52.029 --> 00:07:53.509
of the world tree.

00:07:53.509 --> 00:07:58.479
Yet the people in these images don’t revere
the Nigghogg as something mythic, but rather

00:07:58.479 --> 00:08:04.169
a spectacle to be gawked at — which feels
strangely realistic.

00:08:04.169 --> 00:08:09.499
Taking hoaxes and pieces of folklore and turning
them into something more realistic is a motif

00:08:09.499 --> 00:08:13.199
that appears frequently throughout Valdés-Hevia’s
art.

00:08:13.199 --> 00:08:19.279
For example, “Jenny Hanivers” are hoaxes
created by taking the dried carcass of a ray

00:08:19.279 --> 00:08:23.449
and fashioning it into a fanciful creature
to sell to tourists.

00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:30.150
Yet one piece by Valdés-Hevia imagines a
deep-sea submarine capturing a ‘live specimen’

00:08:30.150 --> 00:08:36.490
on camera — revealing an uncanny and human-like
lifeform staring back from the dark abyss.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:41.959
It’s impressive how the art turns a rather
ridiculous piece of folklore into something

00:08:41.959 --> 00:08:43.120
actually fear-provoking.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:50.750
And Valdés-Hevia isn’t afraid to embrace
absurdity and horror at the same time.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:57.130
One of my favorite pieces shows a trail camera
catching something strange on the 25th of

00:08:57.130 --> 00:08:58.610
December, 2020.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:04.010
The following Christmas, the owner of the
camera hears a horrible cracking sound like

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:07.500
breaking bones coming from their fireplace.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.649
Figuring migratory birds had made a nest inside,
they take a picture up the chimney…

00:09:12.649 --> 00:09:16.370
Valdés-Hevia: “The creepy and the funny
is something really easy to mix together.

00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:18.810
I like doing both.”

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:23.980
One of the best combinations of creepy and
funny comes from the crab person images — a

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:30.010
project in which volunteer test subjects undergo
a procedure intended to evolve their human

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.089
body plans into something more advanced…

00:09:33.089 --> 00:09:34.660
with unexpected results.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:39.790
Valdés-Hevia: “A mad scientist tried to
speed up evolution just to see what mankind

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.990
would evolve into.

00:09:41.990 --> 00:09:44.800
And — oh no — it evolved into crabs.”

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:50.630
This series is a play on carcinization — or
the tendency of crustaceans to evolve into

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:55.700
crabs, an amusing natural phenomenon I’ve
discussed before on the channel.

00:09:55.700 --> 00:10:01.860
So, perhaps the ultimate human would also
have a defensive ribcage shell and a keratinous

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:04.720
claw.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:09.980
Of course, some of Valdés-Hevia’s work
is more overtly terrifying.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:15.880
Returning to the fragmented Megalamorpha series,
the next glimpse of the giant insects’ influence

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:18.420
comes in Norway in 1953.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:25.649
Here, an isolated church becomes a strange
cult, worshiping a mysterious being.

00:10:25.649 --> 00:10:31.100
Journalist Erling Jørgensen is allowed to
visit the community and take pictures, although

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:35.790
he is not permitted to enter the church during
sermon hours.

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:40.370
According to Erling’s notes, however, on
the day of his departure, he is overtaken

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:41.710
by curiosity.

00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:47.740
Sneaking into the church, he sees an immense,
moth-like creature appear on the wall.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:54.300
The congregation is silent as the monstrous
insect pulsates— seemingly under its control.

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:59.410
Valdés-Hevia: “In my Megalamorpha series,
it’s all about these giant insects manipulating

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:01.620
people and kind of brainwashing them.

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:09.149
This one that specializes in releasing pheromones
with this huge organ could brainwash a whole

00:11:09.149 --> 00:11:10.960
group of people.”

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:16.670
After the sermon ends, a fire destroys the
church and the insect that day.

00:11:16.670 --> 00:11:22.361
It is only years later — upon Erling’s
death — that it is revealed that he is the

00:11:22.361 --> 00:11:24.779
one who lit the match…

00:11:24.779 --> 00:11:29.670
But the story of the Megalamorpha doesn’t
end there.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:34.440
More recent discoveries in fact suggest that
the creatures may have been appearing for

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:35.520
centuries.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:40.839
In 1982, a local news channel in Sicily discovers
something bizzare.

00:11:40.839 --> 00:11:47.040
On the wall of a Roman ruin, a mosaic depicts
what looks like an enormous wasp eating an

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.040
antelope.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:52.980
The piece stands out from the other realistic
scenes and does not seem to match any known

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:55.560
Roman myths.

00:11:55.560 --> 00:12:01.450
In a medieval codex from 15th century Spain,
the Megalamorpha seem to appear again.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:07.360
While traveling in a boat, a nobleman’s
son discovers an unusual cocoon in the sea,

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:09.980
which soon begins to alter his mind.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:15.070
When an insect hatches from the cocoon, the
nobleman’s son believes the creature to

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:23.649
be holy, and has numerous statues made in
its honor — all of which have bug-like features.

00:12:23.649 --> 00:12:29.680
But other pieces indicate that human interactions
with the Megalamorpha go back much further.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:35.550
Neolithic paintings in a cave found near Tírig,
Spain depict what appears to be people fighting

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.390
a large six-legged insect.

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:44.610
In the next image, however, people kneel before
a similar figure in a more solemn pose.

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:47.430
They appear to be making offerings to it.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:52.420
The following part seems to depict the next
stage in the insect’s lifecycle, but it

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:57.860
has been purposefully chiseled off — leaving
few recognizable traces.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:02.800
The final scene shows more people kneeling
and laying down their weapons below beings

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:08.500
that show elements of both humans and the
previous six-limbed creatures — an image

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.750
that is perhaps the most unsettling of all.

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:16.750
Near the paintings is a human skull with unknown
malformations.

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:22.319
Taken as a whole, it seems the Megalamorpha’s
lifecycle eventually involves merging with

00:13:22.319 --> 00:13:23.319
human hosts.

00:13:23.319 --> 00:13:28.470
But we’ll come back to that story…

00:13:28.470 --> 00:13:34.720
Across Valdés-Hevia’s gallery of terrors,
another ingeniously bizarre concept is Xipe-Totec

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.740
— a planet seemingly made of living flesh
that passes close to Earth.

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:44.910
There’s something cosmically terrifying
and strangely amusing about this visual — with

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:50.260
veins crisscrossing the planet like rivers,
and spots that resemble eyes where craters

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:51.260
should be.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:55.200
The Archivist: So, what can you tell us about
the planet made of living flesh?

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:00.050
Valdés-Hevia: It does have a little bit of,
you know, speculative biology stuff.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:02.240
As accurate as you can get about, you know,
meat planets.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:09.450
It looks red and meaty, and that is because
it uses a pigment, kind of like chlorophyl,

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.899
to absorb energy from stars.

00:14:11.899 --> 00:14:15.630
And to move around, it needs to expel some
matter…

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:19.480
kind of like, fart around the universe in
a way.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:25.010
In the story that accompanies these images,
after passing close to Earth, the Xipe-Totec

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:29.890
leaves behind fragments torn from its surface
due to earth’s gravitational pull.

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:35.190
Authorities warn people not to approach the
debris — as it still seems to pulse with

00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:38.170
life, which doesn’t bode well.

00:14:38.170 --> 00:14:43.600
And after the planet leaves the solar system,
the probe Voyager 2 takes a space photo of

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:49.129
what appears to be another member of the Xipe-Totecs
species that is now just a skeleton.

00:14:49.129 --> 00:14:53.569
Valdés-Hevia: “So the bone version of the
meat planet that we see was one of these planets

00:14:53.569 --> 00:14:55.370
that didn’t make it.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:15:00.139
It was very unlucky, and it didn’t find
a new star system, and kept roaming around

00:15:00.139 --> 00:15:04.449
until it was just a husk...”

00:15:04.449 --> 00:15:09.910
One of Valdés-Hevia’s longer series is
The Lycanthrope Project — an exploration

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:14.970
of an alternate history with werewolves made
in collaboration with the talented horror

00:15:14.970 --> 00:15:17.209
artist David Romero.

00:15:17.209 --> 00:15:23.430
The mixed media series brilliantly reimagines
the werewolf mythos, reframing the monsters’

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:26.519
relationship to society in unexpected ways.

00:15:26.519 --> 00:15:30.579
The Archivist: “So, what was the vision
behind The Lycanthrope Project?”

00:15:30.579 --> 00:15:36.610
Valdés-Hevia: “One of the driving concepts
we had behind this was putting the Werewolf

00:15:36.610 --> 00:15:39.089
where the vampire usually is.

00:15:39.089 --> 00:15:43.430
So, the vampire usually works as this aristocratic
killer.

00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:50.620
It’s the personification of the nobleman
leeching off the regular people.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:56.050
And removing the vampire from there and putting
a werewolf — which is usually this bestial

00:15:56.050 --> 00:15:57.050
thing.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:16:01.990
It’s not usually a creature that’s in
control.”

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:06.149
One of the earliest depictions of werewolves
in the story of The Lycanthrope Project comes

00:16:06.149 --> 00:16:11.459
from a Roman mosaic, which depicts a party
of hunters killing a lycanthrope.

00:16:11.459 --> 00:16:14.629
The curse of the wolf is passed on through
bloodlines.

00:16:14.629 --> 00:16:20.730
And although in this image the werewolves
are hunted, powers soon shift as individuals

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.230
with the curse rise through the ranks of nobility.

00:16:23.230 --> 00:16:29.620
The next image depiction comes from a Medieval
triptych, commissioned in the twelfth century

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:34.029
by a secret society of knights called the
Order of the New Moon.

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:39.180
This group of werewolves, which features heavily
in The Lycanthrope Project, believe themselves

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:44.870
to be a chosen, holy people — destined to
rule over the masses.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:51.010
Inching closer to the present, an old photograph
shows how in some insular Nordic communities,

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:55.180
old customs of worshiping Lycanthropes are
still maintained.

00:16:55.180 --> 00:17:01.560
In ancient times, these groups practiced initiation
rites that often including wearing masks shaped

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:08.140
like wolf heads — showing how the influence
of werewolves has spread across the globe.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:13.790
In time, the ranks of the secretive Order
of the New Moon open to a select few.

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:19.179
At the dawn of the 20th century, powerful
figures begin intermarrying into the family

00:17:19.179 --> 00:17:23.130
— lured by the promise of joining the Lycan
Bloodline.

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:29.750
Today, the higher ranks of the enigmatic Order
are said to be formed by the rich and powerful

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.350
from across the world.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:35.160
The full extent of their reach, however, is
left a mystery.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:40.860
Like many of Valdés-Hevia’s projects, the
narrative is intentionally open ended, encouraging

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.750
the viewer to fill in the gaps...

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:49.940
And to conclude our look into the mind of
Valdés-Hevia, we’ll return one final time

00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:53.380
to the terrors of the Megalamorpha project.

00:17:53.380 --> 00:17:58.380
Some of the most illuminating fragments of
the narrative are the last surviving photographs

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:04.790
from the secretive De La Cruz family — which
at last offer us a look at a Megalamorpha-human

00:18:04.790 --> 00:18:06.080
hybrid.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:10.880
According to Adrián De La Cruz — a disowned
relative who smuggled the photographs out

00:18:10.880 --> 00:18:16.520
of the family estate — the monster in this
image was known to the family as “Tío Alfredo.”

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:22.680
Adrián claims the creature has always been
there since the time of his great-grandfathers,

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:25.270
having convinced their minds it was part of
the family.

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:30.000
The Archivist: “So, how did you approach
designing the Megalamorpha-human hybrid?”

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:34.700
Valdés-Hevia: “It took me a long time to
kind of figure out how to mix those bug and

00:18:34.700 --> 00:18:35.700
human elements.

00:18:35.700 --> 00:18:43.170
It’s carrying the bones outside — the
human part of it is kind of out in the exoskeleton.”

00:18:43.170 --> 00:18:47.700
According to the account of Adrián De La
Cruz, he planned to bring an end to the creature

00:18:47.700 --> 00:18:51.700
by drinking a bottle of cyanide and letting
it feed upon him.

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:56.070
It is unclear if he was successful…

00:18:56.070 --> 00:19:01.070
In any case, the tale of the Megalamorpha
continues into the modern era.

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:07.340
In 2004, a company charting the sea floor
off the coast of Hawaii discovers an abandoned

00:19:07.340 --> 00:19:09.700
remotely operated vehicle.

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:15.010
The vehicle was lost during the survey of
a sunken ship, with its umbilical cord cut

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:16.010
off.

00:19:16.010 --> 00:19:21.010
And on the vehicle’s camera is a photo of
a familiar insect-like creature which seems

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:25.630
to have been the cause of the vehicle’s
disappearance — and perhaps the wreck of

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:28.720
the ship itself.

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:35.500
The final glimpse of the Megalamorpha comes
in the form of trail cam pictures from 2006

00:19:35.500 --> 00:19:38.760
recovered from a now defunct online hunting
forum.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:43.890
As for what happened to the Megalamorpha from
here, however, is anyone’s guess.

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:50.460
For who can say what lurks in the dark corners
of the world?

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:56.180
This brief glimpse into the striking and surreal
creations of Eduardo Valdés-Hevia leaves

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.400
many questions still unanswered.

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:04.330
But, according to the artist himself, a mystery
is most engaging when there’s a little ambiguity.

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:07.700
Valdés-Hevia: “I’m not one for giving
out the whole lore to everybody because I

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:09.450
think that ruins a little bit of the fun.

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:13.450
It’s that found horror thing where it makes
you dig for more.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:18.770
You are kind of cursing yourself with knowledge,
but you need to know more.”

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:24.200
If you’d like to discover more of this mysterious
art for yourself, there are links in the description.

00:20:24.200 --> 00:20:30.670
A tremendous thank you to Eduardo Valdés-Hevia
for sharing insights into his many projects.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:35.640
